# Data Model Explanation

## Overview

The dashboard is built using a layered modeling approach inspired by dimensional modeling principles.

The architecture separates:

- Raw transactional tables
- Staging (cleaned) tables
- Aggregated metric tables
- Dimension tables for reporting

This design improves clarity, performance, and metric consistency.

---

## Reporting Layer Structure

### Metric Tables (Aggregated)

- m_exec_kpis
- m_monthly_sla
- m_region_sla
- m_seller_sla
- m_customer_state_sla

Each metric table is pre-aggregated at a specific grain to avoid ambiguous filtering inside Power BI.

---

## Dimension Tables

- dim_customer
- dim_seller

Dimensions are used for filtering and slicing, not metric computation.

---

## Modeling Decisions

- Metric tables are intentionally not connected to each other.
- Relationships are kept minimal and single-directional.
- Aggregations are performed in SQL instead of DAX for performance and clarity.
- Each table has a clearly defined grain.

---

## Why This Matters

This approach ensures:

- No circular dependencies
- No ambiguous filter paths
- Predictable KPI calculations
- Clean executive reporting

The model reflects BI developer best practices rather than ad-hoc dashboard construction.
